ts and then sum the results. The function \\( f(n) \\) is defined as follows:\n\\[\nf(n) = \\begin{cases} \n\\lfloor n \\rfloor & \\text{if } n \\geq 4, \\\\\n\\lceil n \\rceil & \\text{if } n < 4.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\( f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) + f(\\sqrt{45}) + f(8^{2/3}) \\).\n\n1. **Evaluate \\( f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\):**\n   - First, calculate \\( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\). Since \\( \\pi \\approx 3.1416 \\), we have \\( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\approx 1.0472 \\).\n   - Since \\( 1.0472 < 4 \\), we use the ceiling function: \\( \\lceil 1.0472 \\rceil = 2 \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = 2 \\).\n\n2. **Evaluate \\( f(\\sqrt{45}) \\):**\n   - First, calculate \\( \\sqrt{45} \\). Since \\( 45 = 9 \\times 5 \\), we have \\( \\sqrt{45} = \\sqrt{9 \\times 5} = 3\\sqrt{5} \\).\n   - Since \\( \\sqrt{5} \\approx 2.236 \\), we have \\( 3\\sqrt{5} \\approx 3 \\times 2.236 = 6.708 \\).\n   - Since \\( 6.708 \\geq 4 \\), we use the floor function: \\( \\lfloor 6.708 \\rfloor = 6 \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( f(\\sqrt{45}) = 6 \\).\n\n3. **Evaluate \\( f(8^{2/3}) \\):**\n   - First, calculate \\( 8^{2/3} \\). Since \\( 8 = 2^3 \\), we have \\( 8^{2/3} = (2^3)^{2/3} = 2^2 = 4 \\).\n   - Since \\( 4 \\geq 4 \\), we use the floor function: \\( \\lfloor 4 \\rfloor = 4 \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( f(8^{2/3}) = 4 \\).\n\nFinally, we sum the results:\n\\[\nf\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) + f(\\sqrt{45}) + f(8^{2/3}) = 2 + 6 + 4 = 12.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]",
